{
  "$comment": [
    "M-030 AC-5 · The address-normaliser corpus.",
    "",
    "WHAT AC-5 ASKS FOR, AND WHAT THIS IS.",
    "AC-5 asks for tuning against '200 real Indian addresses including flat and floor variants'.",
    "These are NOT real addresses. Every one is synthetic, and putting 200 real gym addresses in a",
    "repository would be a BR-DAT-06 problem committed for the sake of a fixture — the same reason",
    "india-identifiers.spec.ts uses synthetic PANs.",
    "",
    "WHY PAIRS RATHER THAN A FLAT LIST.",
    "A list of 200 addresses asserts nothing on its own: it can only show that the normaliser did",
    "not crash. What decides whether BR-GYM-09 works is whether two TYPINGS of one premises collapse",
    "to one string, and whether two genuinely different premises stay apart. So every entry is a",
    "pair with a verdict, and each one is a real assertion that can fail.",
    "",
    "The variant classes are drawn from the milestone's own wording — 'flat, floor and wing variants'",
    "— plus the ones probing found: initialisms (M G Road / MG Road), word ordinals (First Floor /",
    "1st Flr), and comma-delimited landmarks, which ate the locality in the first implementation.",
    "",
    "THE GAP IS RECORDED. A real corpus, drawn from live submissions once there are any, is KL-107.",
    "Until then these cover the SHAPES; they cannot cover the surprises only real data carries."
  ],

